Toward an Adaptive Resolution of the Blockchain Trilemma:

A Formally Grounded Framework with Shielded Reinforcement Learning

Version 4

ouadi Maakoul 


Abstract. 


We present a mathematical framework for adaptive blockchain consensus that navigates scalability, security, decentralisation, and energy as continuous, measurable objectives. The system is modelled as a partially observable stochastic game with five parameterised attack families. A safety shield, synthesised via reactive synthesis from linear temporal logic, enforces correctness invariants, while a separate liveness monitor preserves progress. The consensus policy is trained under a shielded reinforcement learning algorithm; we prove conditional convergence to a local Pareto optimum when the shield intervention probability either vanishes or is bounded by a Lyapunov contractivity condition. For live protocol migration we introduce a rollback‑capable interruptibility operator with formal error bounds on trilemma metrics, and we specify a dual‑mode overlap protocol with a safe arbitration rule. The entire design is accompanied by a verification roadmap in Isabelle/HOL and explicit status of all claims. The framework is purely theoretical; all assumptions and open problems are clearly identified.

---

1. Introduction

The blockchain trilemma asserts fundamental trade‑offs among scalability, security, and decentralisation. Recent work attempts to formalise these trade‑offs as a Pareto frontier rather than a rigid constant‑product bound. We extend this line by adding energy consumption as a fourth dimension, reflecting growing environmental concerns. Our contribution is a fully mathematical framework that integrates shielded reinforcement learning (RL), formal methods, and multi‑objective optimisation to adapt consensus parameters in real time while maintaining provable safety and liveness.

Existing proposals suffer from two critical gaps: (i) they lack formal guarantees that the learning process does not violate consensus correctness, and (ii) they assume idealised conditions for migration between protocols. We address both by constructing a safety shield and a liveness monitor that operate independently of the RL agent, and by designing a rollback‑capable interruptibility operator whose correctness is proven under explicit assumptions.

The paper is organised as follows. Section 2 defines the system model and four operational trilemma metrics with rigorous under‑approximations. Section 3 formulates the stochastic game. Section 4 presents the shielded consensus mechanism and the factored shield synthesis theorem. Section 5 provides the convergence analysis, including a Lyapunov‑based contractivity condition and a neighbourhood convergence theorem. Section 6 details the interruptibility operator with formal guarantees. Section 7 outlines the verification roadmap. Section 8 lists all assumptions and open problems. Appendices contain full proofs and algorithmic specifications.

