2. System Model and Operational Trilemma Metrics

Definition 2.1 (Blockchain System). A blockchain system is a tuple \mathcal{B} = (N, \mathcal{T}, \mathcal{C}, \mathcal{S}, E) where N = \{1,\dots,N\} is the set of nodes, \mathcal{T} the transaction space, \mathcal{C} the consensus mechanism, \mathcal{S} the global state space, and E: \mathcal{S} \to \mathbb{R}_{\ge 0} the energy consumption (joules per epoch). The state evolves as

s_{t+1} = F(s_t, a_t, \xi_t),

with a_t \in \mathcal{A} a consensus action and \xi_t \sim P(\cdot \mid s_t) capturing network delays and adversarial perturbations.

2.1 Operational Metrics

All metrics map \mathcal{S} to [0,1] and are assumed continuously computable.

Scalability: S(s) = \tau(s)/\tau_{\max}, where \tau(s) is the average confirmed transactions per second over a sliding window of W blocks, and \tau_{\max} a hardware bound.

Security:
Let \Phi(s) be the minimal fraction of honest stake an adversary must corrupt to break safety or liveness. We require a safe under‑approximation \widehat{\Phi}(s) \le \Phi(s). For BFT‑class protocols, \widehat{\Phi}_{\text{BFT}}(s) = (N-f)/N.
For Nakamoto PoS, let v_1 \ge v_2 \ge \dots \ge v_n be validator stakes. A reorganisation from depth k requires the adversary to outweigh \lceil k/2 \rceil consecutive honest blocks. The minimum stake to reorg from depth k is the smallest total stake that, combined with the observed fork rate, could build a heavier chain; we approximate

\widehat{\Phi}_{\text{Nakamoto}}(s) = \frac{\text{stake of smallest set controlling } \ge \frac12 \text{ total stake}}{\text{total active stake}}.

For Avalanche (Snowball), with default parameters k=20, \alpha=15, the safety condition is \text{adversary weight} < \alpha - k/2. Hence

\widehat{\Phi}_{\text{Avalanche}}(s) = \max\!\left(0, \frac{k\alpha - k/2}{\text{total weight}}\right).

The security metric is Q(s) = 1 - \widehat{\Phi}(s)/\Phi_0, where \Phi_0 is the nominal fault tolerance (e.g., 1/2 for BFT, 1/2 for longest‑chain PoS under synchrony).

Decentralisation: D(s) = H(s)/\log_2 N, where H(s) is the Shannon entropy of the block‑production shares over the last W blocks.

Energy: We define an abstract energy functional

E(s) = \gamma_1 \cdot M(s) + \gamma_2 \cdot C(s),

where M(s) is the total message complexity (bytes sent) and C(s) the total computational cost (CPU cycles) expended during the epoch. The constants \gamma_1,\gamma_2 are reference‑machine energy coefficients to be calibrated offline. To avoid masking energy‑intensive validators, the system‑wide figure is the 95th percentile of the per‑validator energy consumption. Bounds on this percentile under validator heterogeneity are derived via order statistics (Appendix A.4).

All four metrics are Lipschitz continuous with respect to the state transition function; proofs are sketched in Appendix B.6.

