3. Stochastic Game with Adversarial Taxonomy

The system is modelled as a partially observable stochastic game (POSG) with N agents. Honest nodes share a policy \pi_\theta; the adversary controls up to f nodes, with |N_A|/N \le \beta < 1/2. The adversary selects its attack from five families, each with a bounded budget:

· A1 – Naive Malicious (stateless equivocation, invalid blocks).
· A2 – Collusive Rumor (coordinated false dissemination).
· A3 – Adaptive Adversarial (observes \pi_H and optimises online).
· A4 – Byzantine Fault Injection (state‑correlated faults).
· A5 – Time‑Delayed Poisoning (injects training data harmful after a delay).

The honest objective is to maximise the expected cumulative discounted reward

J(\theta) = \mathbb{E}_{\pi_\theta,\pi_A}\!\left[\sum_{t=0}^\infty \gamma^t r_t\right],

where r_t = \lambda_S S(s_t) + \lambda_Q Q(s_t) + \lambda_D D(s_t) - \lambda_E \varepsilon(s_t). The adversary aims to minimise \mathbb{E}[Q(s_t)] subject to its budget.

