4. Shielded Adaptive Consensus Mechanism

4.1 Safety Shield and Liveness Monitor

The policy \pi_\theta : \mathcal{S} \to \Delta(\mathcal{A}) is filtered by a safety shield \sigma_s : \mathcal{S} \times \mathcal{A} \to \mathcal{A} \cup \{\texttt{reject}\}, synthesised from the LTL property

\varphi_{\text{safety}} = \Box (\neg \text{two\_conflicting\_blocks} \land \text{quorum\_intersection}).

The shield blocks actions that would leave the maximal safe region and falls back to a statically verified default a_{\text{safe}}(s). A separate liveness monitor \mu maintains a round counter; if no block is confirmed within k rounds, it forces a_{\text{safe}}(s). Liveness is inherited from the base protocol under the standard fault bound \beta; a timed LTL proof is outlined in Appendix D.2.

4.2 Factored Shield Synthesis

The action space comprises four dimensions (block size, timeout ratio, commit rule, leader schedule), leading to 96 combinations. Because the safety invariant decomposes into a conjunction of independent properties over disjoint action variables, we synthesise sub‑shields for each variable group.

Theorem 4.1 (Factored Shield Composition). Let \varphi = \varphi_1 \land \varphi_2 where \varphi_1, \varphi_2 are LTL formulas over disjoint action sets \mathcal{A}_1, \mathcal{A}_2. If \sigma_1 is a maximal winning strategy for \varphi_1 and \sigma_2 for \varphi_2, then the product shield (\sigma_1, \sigma_2) is a maximal winning strategy for \varphi.

Proof. (Appendix B.1). The composition preserves maximality because the action variables are independent; no assume‑guarantee reasoning across agents is required, unlike Brorholt et al. (2025).

Continuous state variables are discretised with interval width \delta. The resulting abstraction error is conjectured to be \epsilon(\delta) \le L_F \cdot \delta \cdot T, where L_F is the Lipschitz constant of F and T the horizon; bounding L_F for HotStuff remains open.

4.3 Trust‑Aware Replay Buffer

To mitigate A5, transitions are tagged with an anomaly score from an Isolation Forest operating on the vector (block_interval, mempool_size, peer_count). Samples below a threshold \tau_{\min} are discarded. This is a heuristic; a formally analysable detector based on ellipsoidal confidence regions is discussed in Appendix F.4.

