5. Pareto Frontier and Convergence Analysis

5.1 Feasible Region and Pareto Optimality

Let \Pi be the class of shielded policies. The feasible region

\mathcal{F} = \{ (S^\pi, Q^\pi, D^\pi, -\varepsilon^\pi) \mid \pi \in \Pi \}

is compact under the metric topology induced by the state space. The Pareto frontier \mathcal{P} \subset \mathcal{F} consists of all points not strictly dominated.

5.2 Conditional Convergence with Lyapunov Contractivity

Assumption 5.1 (Lyapunov Contractivity). There exists a function V : \Theta \to \mathbb{R}_{\ge 0} and constants \kappa \in (0,1), C>0 such that for all \theta_t,

\mathbb{E}[p_{\text{int}}(\theta_{t+1})] \le \kappa \cdot p_{\text{int}}(\theta_t) + C \|\theta_{t+1} - \theta^*\|,

where p_{\text{int}}(\theta) is the probability that the shield intervenes under policy \theta.

This assumption captures the idea that as the policy approaches a local optimum \theta^*, the shield becomes progressively unnecessary. It is plausible when the adversary’s adaptivity is bounded—a game‑theoretic condition we explore in §5.4.

Theorem 5.2 (Neighbourhood Convergence). Under Assumption 5.1 and standard PPO conditions (smooth J, unbiased advantages, diminishing step size), the shielded PPO iterates converge to a neighbourhood of a stationary point \theta^*. The neighbourhood radius is \mathcal{O}(\limsup_{t} p_{\text{int}}(\theta_t)). Within this neighbourhood, the four objectives satisfy first‑order Pareto stationarity up to the same tolerance.

Proof sketch. (Appendix B.2). The Lyapunov bound forces p_{\text{int}} to decay geometrically, reducing the distribution shift caused by shielding. The policy gradient then approximates the true gradient of J, and standard PPO analysis applies.

5.3 Intervention‑Aware Scalarisation

To explicitly minimise shield interference, we augment the objective with a fifth dimension: the long‑term intervention rate p_{\text{int}}. The augmented reward becomes

r_t^{\text{aug}} = -\max_{i=1..5} w_i (z_i^* - f_i(s_t)),

where f_5(s) = -p_{\text{int}}(s) (to be maximised). This Chebyshev scalarisation can recover any Pareto‑optimal point on the five‑dimensional frontier, including those with minimal shield overhead.

5.4 Game‑Theoretic Condition for Low Intervention

We analyse the stochastic game between the RL policy and the A3 adversary. When the honest policy includes a regularisation term \lambda_{\text{int}} \cdot p_{\text{int}}(\theta), the adversary’s best response under certain budget constraints yields an equilibrium where p_{\text{int}} \le \epsilon. A formal condition is given in Appendix B.3; the proof relies on the adversary’s limited capacity to sustain attacks at the safety boundary.

