6. Interruptibility Operator with Formal Rollback

Definition 6.1 (Rollback‑Capable Interruptibility Operator).
I : \mathcal{S} \times \mathcal{A} \times \mathcal{P} \to \mathcal{S} \times \mathcal{A} \cup \{\texttt{REJECT}, \texttt{ROLLBACK}\}, where \mathcal{P} is a protocol descriptor.

Algorithm 6.1 (safe_interrupt_with_rollback).

1. Verify that p_{\text{new}} carries machine‑checked proofs of \varphi_{\text{safety}} \land \varphi_{\text{liveness}}.
2. Snapshot current trilemma metrics (S,Q,D,\varepsilon).
3. Simulate transition to p_{\text{new}} on a shadow state s_{\text{sim}}.
4. If Q(s_{\text{sim}}) \ge Q_{\min} + \delta_Q, D(s_{\text{sim}}) \ge D_{\min} + \delta_D, S(s_{\text{sim}}) \le S_{\max} - \delta_S, \varepsilon(s_{\text{sim}}) \le \varepsilon(s)(1+\delta_\varepsilon), then proceed; else REJECT.
5. Broadcast proposal and collect 2f+1 signatures.
6. Migrate to p_{\text{new}} and enter trial period of T_{\text{trial}} epochs.
7. Compare actual metrics against simulated ones; if deviation exceeds \Delta_{\text{tol}}, trigger ROLLBACK to the checkpoint.

The additive error terms (\delta_Q, \delta_D, \delta_S, \delta_\varepsilon) are derived from the Lipschitz constants of the metric functions and the maximum shadow‑real divergence, which is in turn bounded by L_F \cdot \max\_deviation(\xi). Full derivation in Appendix C.2.

Theorem 6.2 (Invariant Preservation with Rollback). Under partial synchrony and bounded message delay, Algorithm 6.1 preserves the trilemma bounds up to the error terms, and either finalises migration within T_{\text{trial}} epochs or safely rolls back.

Proof. (Appendix C.3). The rollback mechanism guarantees that the system reverts to a known safe state if the new protocol does not meet the promised metrics.

