Appendices

Appendix A — Mathematical Background and Metric Formalization

A.1 System Model and Transition Semantics

The blockchain is modeled as a discrete-time stochastic dynamical system over an infinite horizon. The state space \mathcal{S} is a compact metric space that includes all quantities relevant for consensus: ledger state (fork tree), validator set and stakes, recent block times, pending transaction pool, and current protocol parameters. The action space \mathcal{A} is finite, encoding the four consensus dimensions (see §6 of the main text). The transition function

s_{t+1} = F(s_t, a_t, \xi_t)

is measurable, where \xi_t \sim P(\cdot \mid s_t) is a random variable capturing network latency, message drops, and adversarial perturbations. For a fixed honest policy \pi_H and adversary policy \pi_A, the joint policy \pi = (\pi_H,\pi_A) induces a probability measure \mathbb{P}_\pi over trajectories \tau = (s_0, a_0, s_1, a_1, \dots). All expectations \mathbb{E}_\pi are taken with respect to this measure.

The system satisfies the Markov property: the conditional distribution of (s_{t+1}, \xi_{t+1}) given the history up to t depends only on (s_t, a_t).

A.2 Operational Metrics — Detailed Construction

All metrics S, Q, D, \varepsilon : \mathcal{S} \to [0,1] are required to be measurable and Lipschitz continuous with respect to the state (proofs in §A.5). We give the precise formulas.

A.2.1 Scalability S(s)

Let \tau(s) be the average number of transactions confirmed per second over the last W blocks. Let \tau_{\max} be a hardware-dependent maximum throughput (e.g., based on reference CPU and network bandwidth). Then

S(s) = \min\!\left(\frac{\tau(s)}{\tau_{\max}}, 1\right).

The window W is chosen large enough to smooth short-term variance while still reflecting current capacity.

A.2.2 Security Q(s)

Let \Phi(s) be the minimal fraction of honest stake (or weight) an adversary must corrupt to break safety or liveness. The nominal fault tolerance is \Phi_0 (e.g., 1/2 for BFT protocols, 1/2 for longest‑chain PoS under synchrony). We require a safe under‑approximation \widehat{\Phi}(s) \le \Phi(s) computable from the state. Then

Q(s) = 1 - \frac{\widehat{\Phi}(s)}{\Phi_0},

clamped to [0,1].

We provide closed‑form under‑approximations for three important protocol classes.

BFT class (e.g., HotStuff, PBFT). If the protocol tolerates up to f Byzantine nodes out of N with f < N/3 (or f < N/2 under optimal synchrony), we set

\widehat{\Phi}_{\text{BFT}}(s) = \frac{N - f}{N}.

This is exact: an adversary who controls more than f nodes can violate safety.

Nakamoto PoS (longest‑chain). Let the validator stakes be v_1 \ge v_2 \ge \dots \ge v_N with total stake V = \sum_i v_i. The Nakamoto coefficient k_N is the smallest integer such that \sum_{i=1}^{k_N} v_i > V/2. A block at depth k is considered confirmed once it has k blocks on top. For an adversary to revert a transaction confirmed at depth k, it must build a heavier alternative chain from the point of the transaction. A necessary condition is that the adversary controls more stake than the combined stake of the k largest honest validators. A conservative under‑approximation is therefore

\widehat{\Phi}_{\text{Nakamoto}}(s) = \frac{\sum_{i=1}^{k_N} v_i}{V},

where k_N is computed from the current stake distribution. We bound the security margin further by taking k as the current confirmation depth used by clients; the attacker must outweigh at least the top \lceil k/2 \rceil honest validators in expectation. Thus we refine

\widehat{\Phi}_{\text{Nakamoto}}(s) = \min_{j \le N} \frac{\sum_{i=1}^{j} v_i}{V} \quad \text{such that } j \ge \frac{k}{2},

which is an under‑approximation because the adversary may need more than this to sustain a private fork.

Avalanche (Snowball). The protocol uses parameters k (sample size, default 20) and \alpha (quorum threshold, default 15). Safety requires that the adversary controls less than \alpha - k/2 of the total weight. The safe under‑approximation is the linear bound

\widehat{\Phi}_{\text{Avalanche}}(s) = \max\!\left(0, \frac{k\alpha - k/2}{\text{total weight}}\right).

When the adversary weight is below this threshold, the protocol provably remains safe. This formula is conservative, as the actual security margin depends on the precise confidence counters.

Generic fallback. If no analytic formula exists, a monitoring oracle provides a conservative estimate \widehat{\Phi}_{\text{gen}}(s) = 1 - \frac{\text{honest sway}}{\text{total sway}}, where “sway” is a protocol‑specific resource (stake, computation, storage). The oracle must guarantee \widehat{\Phi}_{\text{gen}} \le \Phi.

A.2.3 Decentralisation D(s)

Over the last W blocks, let p_i(s) be the fraction of blocks produced by validator i. The Shannon entropy (in bits) is

H(s) = -\sum_{i=1}^N p_i(s) \log_2 p_i(s),

with the convention 0 \log_2 0 = 0. The maximum possible entropy with N active validators is \log_2 N. We therefore define

D(s) = \frac{H(s)}{\log_2 N}.

When the set of active validators changes (nodes join or leave), both H and \log_2 N adapt accordingly.

A.2.4 Energy \varepsilon(s)

We define an abstract energy functional that decomposes into communication and computation costs.

Let M(s) be the total number of bytes transmitted by all validators during an epoch, and C(s) the total number of CPU cycles consumed by consensus operations (signing, verifying, leader election). The system‑wide energy consumption is

E(s) = \gamma_1 \cdot M(s) + \gamma_2 \cdot C(s),

where \gamma_1 (joules per byte) and \gamma_2 (joules per CPU cycle) are reference‑machine coefficients derived from profiling. To account for hardware heterogeneity, we compute the above for each validator individually and then take the 95th percentile across validators as the representative figure:

E_{\text{sys}}(s) = \text{percentile}_{95}( \{E_i(s)\}_{i=1}^N ).

The use of the 95th percentile, rather than the median, ensures that energy‑intensive validators are not hidden by an efficient majority. If the coefficient of variation (\sigma/\mu) of the E_i exceeds 2, governance is advised to rescale the energy weight \lambda_E in the reward.

The normalised metric is

\varepsilon(s) = \frac{E_{\text{sys}}(s)}{E_{\max}},

where E_{\max} is the maximum permissible energy per epoch (determined by hardware budgets or carbon targets).

We provide a distribution‑free bound on the 95th percentile under the assumption of i.i.d. validator energy draws (though in practice the draws are correlated). For N i.i.d. random variables, the p-th sample quantile has variance \frac{p(1-p)}{N f(x_p)^2}, where f is the density and x_p the true quantile. By order‑statistic theory, the 95th percentile estimate is asymptotically normal; Chebyshev bounds give a worst‑case error independent of the distribution. We adopt a conservative bound: the relative error of E_{\text{sys}}(s) with respect to the true population 95th percentile is less than \delta_E with high probability, for N \ge 30. This bound is sufficient for the migration error terms in Appendix C.

A.3 Lipschitz Continuity of the Operational Metrics

We prove that all four metrics are Lipschitz continuous in the state s under appropriate metrics. This property is used in the migration error analysis and in the shield discretisation.

Assumption A.1 (Smooth Transition). The transition function F(s,a,\xi) is Lipschitz in s for each fixed (a,\xi): \|F(s,a,\xi) - F(s',a,\xi)\| \le L_F \|s - s'\|. Moreover, the probability kernel P(\cdot \mid s) is Lipschitz in total variation distance with constant L_P.

Proposition A.1 (Lipschitz Metrics). Under Assumption A.1, the metrics S, Q, D, \varepsilon are Lipschitz continuous on \mathcal{S}.

Proof sketch.

· S(s) depends on throughput \tau(s), which is a moving average of transaction counts; small changes in state shift the window and can change the count by at most one transaction per block, giving a local Lipschitz constant.
· Q(s) uses \widehat{\Phi}(s), which for BFT is constant between configuration changes; for Nakamoto, the stake distribution and Nakamoto coefficient change slowly; for Avalanche, weights are continuous in stake changes.
· D(s) uses the empirical block producer distribution over a finite window; flipping a single block changes p_i by at most 1/W, resulting in an entropy change bounded by \mathcal{O}(\log N / W).
· \varepsilon(s) aggregates CPU and network usage, which are continuous functions of the state (e.g., number of messages, validation steps).

A full derivation of the Lipschitz constants is lengthy; we denote them by L_S, L_Q, L_D, L_\varepsilon.

A.4 Energy Aggregation via 95th Percentile: Order‑Statistic Guarantees

Let E_1,\dots,E_N be the validator energy consumptions, assumed independent and identically distributed with cumulative distribution G and density g. The 95th percentile is the order statistic E_{(k)} with k = \lceil 0.95 N \rceil. Its asymptotic variance is \frac{0.95 \times 0.05}{N g(q)^2}, where q is the true quantile. For finite N, we can use the nonparametric confidence interval for a quantile: the probability that the j-th order statistic is less than or equal to the true p-th quantile is \sum_{i=j}^N \binom{N}{i} p^i (1-p)^{N-i}. Choosing j such that this sum is \ge 1 - \alpha yields a conservative upper bound on the estimation error. We use this to set the tolerance \delta_\varepsilon in the interruptibility operator.

Because this assumption of independence is strong, we emphasise that the 95th percentile remains a heuristic robustness measure; its formal error bound under arbitrary dependence is more complex (e.g., using Azuma–Hoeffding for martingale differences) and deferred to future work.

A.5 Under‑Approximation Proof for Security Metrics

Proposition A.2 (\widehat{\Phi}_{\text{Nakamoto}} is a safe under‑approximation). For a longest‑chain PoS system, if the adversary controls less than \widehat{\Phi}_{\text{Nakamoto}} fraction of total stake, then any reorganisation of depth k requires the adversary to outweigh the k deepest honest validators, which is impossible because the adversary’s stake is less than the combined stake of those validators. Hence \widehat{\Phi}_{\text{Nakamoto}} \le \Phi.

Proof. The adversary must present a heavier chain than the honest one. The honest chain after k confirmations has been built by a set of validators whose combined stake, by the definition of the Nakamoto coefficient, exceeds half the total. The adversary would need to control at least that much stake. Our approximation uses a smaller set (k/2 validators), thus underestimating the required stake, giving a safe bound. More detailed fork‑analysis yields tighter but still conservative bounds; see [Zhang2022] for a discussion.

Proposition A.3 (\widehat{\Phi}_{\text{Avalanche}} under‑approximation). The Snowball safety proof shows that if the adversary weight w_A satisfies w_A < k\alpha - k/2, then the probability of a safety violation is negligible. Hence any value \widehat{\Phi} with \widehat{\Phi} \le \frac{k\alpha - k/2}{\text{total weight}} is a safe under‑approximation.

Proof. By setting \widehat{\Phi} = \max(0, (k\alpha - k/2)/\text{total}), we guarantee \widehat{\Phi} \le w_A when the adversary is unsafe, and zero otherwise. This satisfies \widehat{\Phi} \le \Phi because the true \Phi is at least the smallest honest weight that guarantees safety.

