Appendix B — Proofs

B.1 Proof of Factored Shield Composition Theorem (Theorem 4.1)

Theorem 4.1. Let \varphi = \varphi_1 \land \varphi_2 where \varphi_1 and \varphi_2 are LTL formulas over disjoint action sets \mathcal{A}_1, \mathcal{A}_2 (i.e., the atomic propositions in \varphi_i involve only variables controlled by actions in \mathcal{A}_i). Let \mathcal{M} be the state machine abstraction. If \sigma_1 is a maximal winning strategy (shield) for the safety game of \varphi_1, and \sigma_2 similarly for \varphi_2, then the product shield (\sigma_1,\sigma_2) applied sequentially (reject if any sub‑shield rejects) is a maximal winning strategy for \varphi.

Proof.

1. Safety preservation. Since the properties are over disjoint action variables, an action (a_1,a_2) that is safe for \mathcal{A}_1 according to \sigma_1 (i.e., preserves \varphi_1 from the current state) and safe for \mathcal{A}_2 according to \sigma_2 will, by conjunction, preserve \varphi. This uses the fact that the effects of actions in \mathcal{A}_1 and \mathcal{A}_2 are independent; the state update F(s, (a_1,a_2), \xi) can be decomposed as two sequential updates (or parallel updates that do not interfere). Therefore, (\sigma_1,\sigma_2) never enables a transition that can violate \varphi.
2. Maximality. A maximal winning strategy allows all actions that keep the system in the winning region. Suppose there exists an action (a_1,a_2) that is not rejected by any sub‑shield individually but is rejected by the product shield because one sub‑shield rejects—but by definition, each sub‑shield rejects only actions that would leave its own winning region. Since the global winning region for \varphi is the intersection of the winning regions for \varphi_1 and \varphi_2 (because both must be satisfied), an action that stays inside both individually is guaranteed to stay inside the intersection. Conversely, if an action is allowed by the product, it is allowed by each sub‑shield, so it stays in each winning region, hence in the intersection. The product shield thus enables exactly the set of actions that preserve the global safe set. No larger permissive set exists that preserves safety, so the product is maximal.

The decomposition over action dimensions avoids the need for assume‑guarantee reasoning because the sub‑shields share the same state; they do not control separate agents with partial observability.

B.2 Proof of Neighbourhood Convergence Theorem (Theorem 5.2)

We restate the theorem with precise assumptions.

Assumptions for PPO:

· The value function V_\phi and policy \pi_\theta are parameterised by neural networks with smooth activations.
· The reward r_t is bounded in [-R_{\max}, R_{\max}].
· The step size schedule \alpha_t satisfies \sum_t \alpha_t = \infty, \sum_t \alpha_t^2 < \infty.
· The advantage estimates \hat{A}_t are unbiased.

Assumption 5.1 (Lyapunov Contractivity). There exists a function V_p : \Theta \to \mathbb{R}_{\ge 0} and constants \kappa \in (0,1), C > 0 such that for any sequence of policy parameters generated by PPO updates,

\mathbb{E}\bigl[V_p(\theta_{t+1}) \mid \theta_t\bigr] \le \kappa \cdot V_p(\theta_t) + C \|\theta_{t+1} - \theta^*\|,

where we define V_p(\theta) := p_{\text{int}}(\theta) – the probability that the shield intervenes under policy \theta. (In practice, we may take V_p as a smooth upper bound.)

Theorem 5.2. Under the above assumptions, if the policy gradient converges (i.e., \|\nabla_\theta J(\theta_t)\| \to 0), then \theta_t converges to a neighbourhood of a stationary point \theta^*. The radius of this neighbourhood is \mathcal{O}(\limsup_t p_{\text{int}}(\theta_t)). Within this neighbourhood, the vector of the four objectives satisfies first‑order Pareto stationarity up to an error of the same order.

Proof sketch.

We begin with the standard PPO surrogate objective. The gradient of the expected return can be expressed as

\nabla_\theta J(\theta) = \mathbb{E}_{(s,a) \sim d^{\theta}} \bigl[ \nabla_\theta \log \pi_\theta(a|s) \, Q^{\theta}(s,a) \bigr],

where d^{\theta} is the stationary distribution of the shielded Markov chain (the chain induced by the effective policy \tilde{\pi}_\theta = \sigma_s \circ \pi_\theta). Due to shielding, the effective policy differs from the raw policy: \tilde{\pi}_\theta(a|s) = \sum_{a'} \pi_\theta(a'|s) \mathbf{1}_{\{\sigma_s(s,a')=a\}} + \mathbf{1}_{\{a = a_{\text{safe}}\}} \cdot p_{\text{rej}}(s), where p_{\text{rej}}(s) is the probability that \pi_\theta proposes an action that is rejected (and the shield falls back to a_{\text{safe}}). That rejection probability is exactly p_{\text{int}}(s;\theta); the overall shield intervention rate is p_{\text{int}}(\theta) = \mathbb{E}_{s \sim d^{\theta}}[p_{\text{int}}(s;\theta)].

The gradient of the unshielded objective would use the distribution induced by \pi_\theta; the actual gradient \nabla_\theta J is with respect to the shielded distribution. By the policy gradient theorem,

\nabla_\theta J(\theta) = \mathbb{E}_{s \sim d^{\tilde{\pi}_\theta}, a \sim \tilde{\pi}_\theta} \bigl[ \nabla_\theta \log \tilde{\pi}_\theta(a|s) \, Q^{\tilde{\pi}_\theta}(s,a) \bigr].

When p_{\text{int}} is small, \tilde{\pi}_\theta is close to \pi_\theta in total variation distance, uniformly over states where the shield does not reject. The bias introduced by shielding can be bounded by a term proportional to p_{\text{int}}(\theta). Formally, let g_{\text{true}} = \nabla_\theta J_{\text{unshielded}} (assuming no shield). Then

\| \nabla_\theta J(\theta) - g_{\text{true}} \| \le L \cdot p_{\text{int}}(\theta),

for some constant L depending on the maximum gradient and the Lipschitz constant of the dynamics.

Now, Assumption 5.1 ensures that V_p(\theta_t) = p_{\text{int}}(\theta_t) satisfies a contraction inequality. Because \|\theta_{t+1} - \theta^*\| becomes small as the policy converges (the gradient norm shrinks), the second term C\|\theta_{t+1} - \theta^*\| can be absorbed into a smaller order. Thus \mathbb{E}[p_{\text{int}}(\theta_t)] \to 0 at a geometric rate.

With diminishing intervention, the gradient \nabla_\theta J(\theta_t) converges to the true unshielded gradient, so the PPO updates asymptotically behave as if no shield were present. Standard PPO convergence results [Schulman2017, Alshiekh2017] then imply almost sure convergence to a stationary point of J.

However, because p_{\text{int}} may not reach exactly zero, the limit will be within a neighbourhood of a true stationary point, with radius \mathcal{O}(\limsup p_{\text{int}}). At a stationary point of the weighted sum, the necessary condition for Pareto optimality (KKT conditions) holds: \sum_i w_i \nabla J_i = 0. If the gradient is only approximately zero, the weighted sum condition is satisfied up to a tolerance of \mathcal{O}(p_{\text{int}}). Therefore the four objectives are locally Pareto stationary within that tolerance.

B.3 Game‑Theoretic Condition for Low Intervention

We sketch the analysis of the game between the honest policy (with an added intervention penalty) and the adaptive adversary A3.

Honest agent’s reward is augmented:

\tilde{r}_t = r_t - \lambda_{\text{int}} \cdot \mathbf{1}_{\text{intervention at } t}.

The adversary tries to minimise Q(s_t) over time and can force the system to states where the shield intervenes frequently. We consider a simplified zero‑sum game over the intervention rate: the adversary’s action set is the attack strength, which can push the state toward the safety boundary. The honest agent can adjust \pi_\theta to stay farther from the boundary, trading off some performance.

Lemma B.1. If the adversary operates under a budget constraint B on the total cost of attacks, and the honest agent’s policy class is rich enough to maintain a safety margin, then for sufficiently large \lambda_{\text{int}}, the game has an equilibrium where the expected intervention rate is at most \epsilon, with \epsilon = O(1/\lambda_{\text{int}}).

Proof idea. The honest agent can always choose a conservative policy that guarantees zero interventions (e.g., always propose a_{\text{safe}}). By deviating from that safe policy, it gains performance but risks interventions. The optimal balance, given the adversary’s optimal response, leads to an intervention rate that vanishes as the penalty weight increases. A formal convergence to a Nash equilibrium requires standard fixed‑point arguments under continuity and compactness; we omit the full equilibrium existence proof, which is outside the scope.

The key takeaway is that adding the intervention penalty into the objective makes it possible to satisfy Assumption 5.1, at least for bounded adaptivity.

B.4 Liveness Inheritance Proof (Timed LTL)

We provide a sketch of the proof that the shielded system preserves liveness.

Base protocol property: Under partial synchrony (global stabilisation time GST), the base consensus algorithm satisfies \Box\Diamond \text{block\_confirmed}. That is, from any state, eventually a new block is confirmed.

Monitor behaviour: The liveness monitor \mu maintains a counter c of the number of rounds (each round corresponds to one consensus decision epoch). If c reaches k (a predetermined threshold), the monitor forces the action a_{\text{safe}} in the current round and resets c.

Lemma B.2. If from any state, repeatedly applying a_{\text{safe}} guarantees that a new block is confirmed within at most k rounds (the base protocol’s liveness guarantee under synchrony), then the shielded system satisfies \Box\Diamond \text{block\_confirmed}.

Proof. Trace by contradiction: suppose there is a trajectory where after GST, no block is confirmed forever. The monitor will periodically force a_{\text{safe}}. By the base protocol’s property, within at most k consecutive rounds of a_{\text{safe}}, a block must be confirmed. Therefore infinite non‑confirmation is impossible.

We need to formally ensure that the base protocol’s liveness proof applies even when the RL agent occasionally proposes actions that the shield rejects. However, those rejected actions are overwritten by a_{\text{safe}} immediately, so from the perspective of the consensus protocol, the sequence of executed actions is a mix of shielded‑approved actions and monitor‑forced safe actions. We must argue that the safe actions alone are sufficient to break any liveness deadlock. The base protocol’s liveness typically relies on the existence of an honest supermajority that eventually converges; our monitor ensures that, when progress stalls, all honest nodes fall back to the default safe actions that are known to guarantee convergence. Hence liveness is preserved.

A full mechanisation in Isabelle/HOL would require a formal timed LTL semantics and an explicit proof of this property; we provide an outline in Appendix D.

B.5 Metric Continuity Proofs

We provide a brief proof that S, Q, D, \varepsilon are Lipschitz.

Lemma B.3. For any two states s, s', |S(s) - S(s')| \le \frac{L_\tau}{\tau_{\max}} \|s - s'\|, where L_\tau is the Lipschitz constant of the throughput function \tau.

Proof. \tau(s) depends on the number of confirmed transactions in a sliding window. A small perturbation in state changes the window boundary, altering the count by at most the maximum transaction rate per block times the number of blocks affected, which is proportional to the state shift. Since S is just a scaling, the bound follows.

Lemma B.4. Q(s) is piecewise Lipschitz. In BFT protocols, it changes only at epoch boundaries; in Nakamoto PoS, the stake distribution changes slowly; in Avalanche, weights are recomputed periodically.

Proof. The under‑approximation \widehat{\Phi} involves maxima of stake sums; these are Lipschitz in the stake vector, which is part of state. The overall Q then has a Lipschitz constant.

Lemma B.5. D(s) is Lipschitz with constant O(\frac{\log N}{W}).

Proof. Entropy H(s) = -\sum p_i \log p_i. A change of one block assignment alters the empirical distribution by at most 1/W in two bins, resulting in an entropy change bounded by O(1/W). Normalising by \log_2 N gives the constant.

Lemma B.6. \varepsilon(s) is Lipschitz because M(s) and C(s) are sums of per‑validator contributions that are continuous in state variables.

These lemmas together imply that there exist global Lipschitz constants L_S, L_Q, L_D, L_\varepsilon for the entire state space, provided the stake distribution and resource usage vary slowly. This underpins the error bounds in interruptibility.

B.6 Pareto Stationarity under Chebyshev Scalarisation

The Chebyshev scalarised reward is r_t^{\text{cheb}} = -\max_i w_i (z_i^* - f_i(s_t)). The objective J_{\text{cheb}}(\theta) = \mathbb{E}[\sum \gamma^t r_t^{\text{cheb}}]. This is non‑linear but piecewise linear in the objectives. Under standard conditions, the policy gradient method converges to a stationary point of J_{\text{cheb}}. A necessary condition for Pareto optimality of a vector \mathbf{J} is that no direction simultaneously increases all objectives; that is equivalent to the stationarity of the Chebyshev scalarisation for some weights and ideal points. Therefore, by recovering all stationary points of all Chebyshev problems (by sweeping weights), the algorithm can trace the entire Pareto front [Hairi2025]. We state this as a corollary.

