Appendix C — Interruptibility Operator: Formal Rollback and Error Bounds

C.1 Algorithm with Rollback (Extended)

We present the refined interruptibility operator that uses a trial period and rollback.

Algorithm C.1: safe_interrupt_with_rollback(s_t, p_new, T_trial, Δ_tol)

1. Verify proofs: Check that p_new provides a machine‑checked proof of \varphi_{\text{safety}} \land \varphi_{\text{liveness}}. (In practice, a proof certificate is stored on‑chain.)
2. Snapshot: Compute the current metrics vector m_{\text{pre}} = (S(s_t), Q(s_t), D(s_t), \varepsilon(s_t)).
3. Shadow simulation: Run a simulation of the new protocol on a copy of the state, producing s_{\text{sim}} and metrics m_{\text{sim}} = (S_{\text{sim}}, Q_{\text{sim}}, D_{\text{sim}}, \varepsilon_{\text{sim}}).
4. Pre‑migration check: Ensure the simulated metrics satisfy
   Q_{\text{sim}} \ge Q_{\min} + \delta_Q, \quad D_{\text{sim}} \ge D_{\min} + \delta_D, \quad
   S_{\text{sim}} \le S_{\max} - \delta_S, \quad \varepsilon_{\text{sim}} \le \varepsilon_{\text{pre}}(1+\delta_\varepsilon).
   The margins \delta are derived below.
5. Broadcast and collect votes: Propose the migration with (p_{\text{new}}, m_{\text{sim}}). Wait for a supermajority of 2f+1 validators.
6. Atomic migration: Switch the consensus module to p_{\text{new}} and set the trial counter t_{\text{trial}} = 0. Store a clean checkpoint of the pre‑migration state.
7. Trial phase: For t = 1 to T_{\text{trial}} epochs:
   · Run the new protocol.
   · At the end of each epoch, compute actual metrics m_t.
   · If \| m_t - m_{\text{sim}} \|_{\infty} > \Delta_{\text{tol}}, trigger ROLLBACK.
8. Finalisation: If the trial period completes without rollback, finalise the migration; discard the old checkpoint.

C.2 Derivation of Error Margins \delta

We derive the additive margins used in the pre‑migration check.

The shadow simulation is not exact. The real execution will diverge from the simulation due to adversarial perturbations and modelling inaccuracies. Let \Delta_{\text{shadow}} be an upper bound on the one‑step divergence in state norm: \| s_{\text{real}} - s_{\text{sim}} \| \le \Delta_{\text{shadow}}. Under the Lipschitz continuity of the metrics, the error in each metric can be bounded:

|Q_{\text{real}} - Q_{\text{sim}}| \le L_Q \cdot \Delta_{\text{shadow}}, \quad
|D_{\text{real}} - D_{\text{sim}}| \le L_D \cdot \Delta_{\text{shadow}},

|S_{\text{real}} - S_{\text{sim}}| \le L_S \cdot \Delta_{\text{shadow}}, \quad
|\varepsilon_{\text{real}} - \varepsilon_{\text{sim}}| \le L_\varepsilon \cdot \Delta_{\text{shadow}}.

To guarantee that the actual metrics remain within the user‑specified bounds after migration, we require stricter pre‑migration checks by adding these worst‑case errors as margins:

\delta_Q = L_Q \cdot \Delta_{\text{shadow}}, \quad \delta_D = L_D \cdot \Delta_{\text{shadow}}, \quad
\delta_S = L_S \cdot \Delta_{\text{shadow}}, \quad \delta_\varepsilon = L_\varepsilon \cdot \Delta_{\text{shadow}} / \varepsilon_{\text{pre}}.

The shadow simulation is run under worst‑case (but bounded) adversarial noise to obtain \Delta_{\text{shadow}}. In practice, we may use statistical bounds (high confidence intervals) instead of worst case, but for safety we stay conservative.

The rollback tolerance \Delta_{\text{tol}} should be larger than \delta to avoid spurious rollbacks while still detecting genuine divergence; we set \Delta_{\text{tol}} = c \cdot \max(\delta_Q, \delta_D, \dots) with c > 1.

C.3 Proof of Invariant Preservation with Rollback (Theorem 6.2)

Theorem 6.2. Under the assumptions of Algorithm C.1, and assuming that the rollback can restore the checkpoint state exactly, the migration process either finalises with the actual metrics within the user‑specified bounds (up to the tolerances \delta), or it safely rolls back to the pre‑migration state.

Proof.

· Safety during trial: The new protocol has a machine‑checked proof of safety, so during the trial phase safety is not violated (assuming the proof’s assumptions hold). The rollback is a state‑machine reversion; as long as the checkpoint is intact and no conflicting blocks were finalised during the trial, safety holds. The dual‑mode overlap protocol (if used) ensures that the old chain is not abandoned until finalisation.
· Trilemma bounds: By construction, the pre‑migration check uses strict thresholds with margins \delta. Even if the simulation error is maximal, the actual metrics will satisfy the original bounds (without margins) because the worst‑case deviation is compensated by the margins. Formally:

Q_{\text{real}} \ge Q_{\text{sim}} - \delta_Q \ge (Q_{\min} + \delta_Q) - \delta_Q = Q_{\min},

and similarly for the others.

· Rollback correctness: If during the trial the actual metrics deviate beyond \Delta_{\text{tol}}, this indicates a failure of the simulation assumptions. The system reverts to the checkpoint, which is known to be safe and within the original bounds (since it was the pre‑migration state). The rollback mechanism requires a consensus protocol that can agree on the reversion; this is feasible under the same BFT assumptions as used for normal operation, because the number of faulty nodes remains bounded. Therefore the trilemma bounds are preserved either in the new protocol (migrated) or by returning to the old one.

This proof relies on the exact restoration of the checkpoint; practical implementations would use state machine replication logs to guarantee this.

C.4 Dual‑Mode Overlap Protocol (Specification Sketch)

To avoid a hard switch during migration, we propose a dual‑mode overlap where validators run both protocols for a period. Each block proposal includes a flag indicating which protocol it follows. A safe arbitration rule ensures that safety is not violated if the two protocols produce conflicting blocks. For example, we can enforce that blocks from the new protocol are built on top of the old chain until a migration point, after which the old protocol ceases to produce blocks. Full specification is beyond the scope of this appendix.

