Appendix E — Algorithmic Specifications

E.1 Shielded Markov Decision Process (MDP) Formulation

The effective MDP is \mathcal{M}_{\text{shielded}} = (\mathcal{S}, \mathcal{A}, \tilde{P}, \tilde{R}, \gamma) where

\tilde{P}(s' \mid s, a) = 
\begin{cases}
P_{\text{env}}(s' \mid s, a) & \text{if } \sigma_s(s,a) \ne \text{reject}, \\
P_{\text{env}}(s' \mid s, a_{\text{safe}}(s)) & \text{if } \sigma_s(s,a) = \text{reject},
\end{cases}

and \tilde{R}(s,a,s') is defined similarly. The policy gradient is taken with respect to \pi_\theta, but the trajectory is sampled from \tilde{P}. This formulation is used in the convergence analysis.

E.2 Trust‑Aware Replay Buffer with Ellipsoidal Confidence Regions

We specify an anomaly detector with probabilistic guarantees.

Input: A stream of transition vectors x_t = (\text{block\_interval}, \text{mempool\_size}, \text{peer\_count}). Assume that under normal operation, these follow a multivariate Gaussian distribution with unknown parameters \mu, \Sigma. We maintain online estimates \hat{\mu}, \hat{\Sigma} using exponential moving averages.

Anomaly score: a_t = (x_t - \hat{\mu})^T \hat{\Sigma}^{-1} (x_t - \hat{\mu}). This is the Mahalanobis distance.

Threshold: Under the Gaussian assumption, a_t \sim \chi^2_3. We set a threshold \tau = \chi^2_{3,0.99} (the 99th percentile). If a_t > \tau, the transition is flagged and discarded.

Guarantee: The false positive rate is 1% (i.e., 1% of normal transitions are discarded). For false negatives (poisoned samples undetected), we need additional assumptions about the attacker’s distribution relative to the normal data. By a robust statistics argument, if the adversary’s data lies outside the convex hull of normal training data with high probability, the Mahalanobis detector will flag it with high power. Formal bounds follow from the properties of the Minimum Covariance Determinant estimator, which we adopt for robust \hat{\Sigma}.

We recognise that the Gaussian assumption is idealised; in practice, we would use a non‑parametric extension.

E.3 Intervention‑Aware Scalarisation Algorithm

We compute the scalarised reward r_t_aug = - max( w1*(z1 - S), w2*(z2 - Q), w3*(z3 - D), w4*(z4 + ε), w5*(z5 + p_int) ) where z5 is the ideal (minimum) intervention rate, typically zero. The estimate of p_int at time t is the frequency of shield rejections over the last H steps.

During PPO updates, we treat this scalar reward as the signal.

E.4 Full PPO with Shielding and Trust‑Aware Buffer Pseudocode

```
Algorithm: Shielded PPO with Trust‑Aware Replay Buffer

Input: initial policy θ, critic ϕ, shield σ_s, monitor μ, hyperparameters
Output: trained θ

Initialise replay buffer B with capacity C
Initialise IF detector D or Mahalanobis estimator M

for episode = 1 to M do
    s = initial_state()
    for t = 1 to T do
        a_raw ∼ π_θ(· | s)
        a = σ_s(s, a_raw)
        if a == REJECT then a = a_safe(s)
        if μ.trigger(s) then a = a_safe(s); μ.reset()
        s' = step(s, a)
        r = compute_reward(s, s')   // optionally Chebyshev or linear
        trust_score = anomaly_detector(s, a, s')
        B.store(s, a, r, s', trust_score)
        if B.size >= batch_size then
            minibatch = B.sample_trusted(threshold τ_min)
            compute advantages A_t via GAE on minibatch
            Update θ using PPO clipped objective on minibatch
            Update ϕ via MSE on returns
        end if
        s = s'
    end for
    if episode % retrain_interval == 0 then
        anomaly_detector.retrain(B.all_normal_samples())
    end if
end for
```

